{"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":0,"metadata":{"colab":{"provenance":[{"file_id":"1CylgvR2yLUhh3Pfa8XKUSsiJJ6Spsk1v","timestamp":1711750687177},{"file_id":"1Gz2WbAyXN5EJUZlmo6H3IN7NIhUByl4f","timestamp":1711488901843}],"toc_visible":true},"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"Python 3"}},"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Simulating Chemical Kinetics\n","\n","In Chem 42, you have seen the simplest textbook examples of zeroth, first, and second order reactions. However, sometimes the reaction kinetics is not as simple as we hope it to be. For example, in biological systems, the number of molecules might be so small that the reaction hardly follows the rate laws. In such a complex situation, we will have to **simulate** the reaction to learn how the chemical reaction proceeds."],"metadata":{"id":"dsn6JSD2IlMt"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["import multiprocessing\n","import tqdm\n","\n","import numpy as np\n","import scipy.stats as st\n","import numba\n","\n","\n","\n","# Plotting modules\n","import bokeh.io\n","import bokeh.plotting\n","\n","bokeh.io.output_notebook()\n"],"metadata":{"id":"6PhRkNi6yYTb"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## **Sampling a population**\n","\n"," The simulation usually starts by creating a **sample**. Sampling involved using a random number generator to simulate the story that generates the distribution. So, if you know the story and you have a computer handy, you can sample to be able to get a plot of your distribution, though you will not get the analytical form."],"metadata":{"id":"IQNBDUihw_fL"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["As a simplest example, let's simulate a coin flip and compare the result to the theoretical distributions."],"metadata":{"id":"EwBjZqUFynT7"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["def simulate_coinflips(n, p, size=1):\n","    \"\"\"\n","    Simulate n_samples sets of n coin flips with prob. p of heads.\n","    \"\"\"\n","    n_heads = np.empty(size, dtype=int)\n","    for i in range(size):\n","        n_heads[i] = np.sum(np.random.random(size=n) < p)\n","    return n_heads\n","\n","size = (30, 100, 1000, 10000)\n","n = 25\n","p = 0.25\n","h_plot = np.arange(26)\n","theor_dist = st.binom.pmf(h_plot, n, p)\n","\n","# Here, I have used the bokeh package to plot, since it is a convenient way to get nice plots\n","# You may also use matplotlib instead\n","plots = []\n","for n_samp in size:\n","    h = simulate_coinflips(n, p, size=n_samp)\n","\n","    plot = bokeh.plotting.figure(height=200,\n","                                 width=300,\n","                                 x_axis_label='h',\n","                                 y_axis_label='P(h)',\n","                                 title=f'{n_samp} samples')\n","    plot.circle(h_plot, theor_dist)\n","    plot.circle(np.arange(h.max()+1), np.bincount(h)/n_samp, color='orange')\n","    plots.append(plot)\n","\n","bokeh.io.show(bokeh.layouts.gridplot(plots, ncols=2))"],"metadata":{"id":"Og9zawImyOh_"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["As we can see, sampling allows us to approximately calculate the actual distribution. If we sample enough, the approximation is very good.\n","\n","Sampling is a very powerful strategy of computing that is happening in a population of molecules (you may apply the idea to a population of people, too, but we will focus on molecules and chemical reactions today.)\n","\n","We will use the same strategy for solving the kinetics of chemical reactions. This technique was pioneered by Dan Gillespie in the last 70s. For that reason, these sampling techniques are often called **Gillespie Algorithm**. The algorithm is sometimes referred to as a **stochastic simulation algorithm**, or SSA. The Gillespie Algorithm is the grand-daddy of stochastic simulation methods!\n","\n","The Gillespie algorithm is one of the most historically important stochastic simulation algorithms ever created. At its heart the intuition behind it is very simple and it is re-assuring that it \"works\" - this is not always the case with stochastic simulation where the \"obvious\" idea can sometimes have unintended debilitating consequences.\n","\n","The algorithm was first presented by Doob (and is sometimes refered to as the Doob-Gillespie algorithm) in the mid 1940s. It was implemented by Kendall in the 1950s. However it wasn't until the mid 1970s that Gillespie re-derived the method by studying physical systems that it became widely used. In publishing the method he essentially created the entire fields of systems biology and computational chemistry by opening the door to what is possible through stochastic simulation (Source: [Lewis Cole Blog](https://lewiscoleblog.com/gillespie-algorithm)).\n","\n","Today, we will explore how this algorithm works by looking at simple chemical reactions -- that was the application that Gillespie originally had in mind when he created the algorithm!\n","\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"yVW8qqtu3S8j"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## A Simple Reaction\n","\n","To illustrate the algorithm in action we will take a simple reaction. We will have following forward reaction where two monomers $A$ and $B$ react to form a dimer $AB$:\n","\n","$$ A + B \\longrightarrow  AB $$\n","\n","The corresponding reverse reaction is the dissociation of the dimer into monomers:\n","\n","$$ AB \\longrightarrow  A + B $$\n","\n","Let's imagine all the spieces are in a beaker and we can count the number of them. Let's denote the number of the species by $N_A$, $N_B$, and $N_{AB}$.\n","\n","We will denote the rate constant of the forward reaction by $k_f$ and that of the backward reaction by $k_b$. The rate of the forward reaction is then\n","\n","$$r_f = k_f N_A N_B$$\n","\n","and the rate of the backward reaction is\n","\n","$$r_b = k_b N_{AB}$$\n","\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"xCdJdR5W9qDA"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["##### Parameter for the reaction #####\n","kf = 2        # Forward reaction rate\n","kb = 1        # Backwards reaction rate"],"metadata":{"id":"UwBauYKJdOME"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### The state of the system\n","\n","The Gillespie Algorithm deals with how the system transition from one **state** to another. Here, a *state* in a beaker would be specified by the number of molecules $N_A$, $N_B$, and $N_{AB}$. Specifically, the algorithm looks at how the system transition from one state to another, for example, by adding a dimer and removing a molecule of $A$ and $B$ each if the forward reaction has happened.\n","\n","\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"PbQzaTQs-Wxa"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["###### Define the initial state of the system ######\n","N_A0 = 25     # Initial number of A molecules\n","N_B0 = 35     # Initial number of B molecules\n","N_AB0 = 5     # Initial number of AB molecules\n","\n","##### Current State (this will be updated during the reaction) #####\n","N_A = N_A0\n","N_B = N_B0\n","N_AB = N_AB0"],"metadata":{"id":"Cs2lB225c_0G"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Time between reactions\n","\n","When reaction occurs, the system transitions from one state to another. This is an **event**, meaning something has happened. In the Gillespie Algorithm, the probability for the event to occur is according to the rate of the reaction.\n","\n","In this example, the rate of an event happening (either forward of backward reaction) is the sum of the forward reaction rate and the backward reaction rate:\n","\n","$$ R = k_f N_A N_B + k_b N_{AB} $$\n","\n","For such systems, the time taken for the arrival of the next reaction event typically follows a Poisson Process. (If you are in a country --- not here --- that does have some public transport and if you are waiting at the bus stop, the time it takes for the next bus to arrive follows a Poisson Process, too.) Under this assumption, the time period $\\Delta T$ between two events can be sampled by $\\tau = \\frac{1}{R} \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)$, where $u$ is a uniformly distributed random number between 0 and 1.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"qDjGRtzyCCLA"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Calculate updated overall reaction rate\n","R = kf * N_A * N_B + kb * N_AB\n","\n","# Calculate time to next reaction\n","u = np.random.random()\n","tau = 1/R * np.log(1/u)"],"metadata":{"id":"tNQQc-dgeQYX"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Practice Problem:** Try to sample `tau` many times. How does the distribution look like? What if you increase or decrease the number of trials?"],"metadata":{"id":"nCxOExxcfLJu"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["nTrials = 1000\n","tauTrials = np.zeros(nTrials)\n","\n","for i in range(nTrials):\n","  u = np.random.random()\n","  tau = 1/R * np.log(1/u)\n","  tauTrials[i] = tau\n","\n","plot = bokeh.plotting.figure(height=400,\n","                             width=600,\n","                             title=\"Time between events\",\n","                             background_fill_color=\"#fafafa\",\n","                             x_axis_label='τ',\n","                             y_axis_label='frequency')\n","\n","hist, edges = np.histogram(tauTrials, bins=50)\n","\n","plot.quad(top=hist, bottom=0, left=edges[:-1], right=edges[1:],\n","       fill_color=\"navy\", line_color=\"white\", alpha=0.5)\n","\n","bokeh.plotting.show(plot)\n"],"metadata":{"id":"D_bpdq0xfAGj"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Propensity\n","\n","And then, given there is a reaction, the probability that it is a forward reaction is\n","\n","$$ p_f = \\frac{k_f N_A N_B}{R}$$\n","\n","and the probability that it is a backward reaction is\n","\n","$$ p_b = \\frac{k_b N_{AB}}{R}$$\n","\n","These are called the *propensity* of different events.\n","\n","Using the same analogy of waiting for the bus. Let's say there are two buses coming to the bus stop. The schedule of Bus A is twice as frequent as Bus B. Then, at the bus stop, when a bus comes, it is twice as likely being Bus A than Bus B."],"metadata":{"id":"1S920F9Ubpze"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Select which reaction to occur\n","pf = kf * N_A * N_B/ R # probability of forward reaction\n","\n","u2 = np.random.random() # generate a random number between 0 and 1\n","\n","# Update populations\n","if u2 < pf:\n","  print(\"forward reaction occurs\")\n","\n","else:\n","  print(\"backward reaction occurs\")"],"metadata":{"id":"j1vhH84gkj2K"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### The Gillespie Algorithm\n","\n","The Gillespie algorithm is allows us to model the exact dynamics described by the master equation. In some (simple) cases we can solve the master equation analytically, but for complicated examples (e.g. say we have 50 different types of reaction occuring) this may not be feasible and so the Gillespie algorithm (or some sort of simulation method) is necessary. In pseudo code we can write down the Gillespie algorithm as:\n","\n","\n","\n","1.   **Initialization** - initialize the system, in the context of reaction kinetics this amounts to the setting up initial chemical concentrations\n","\n","2.   **Monte-Carlo**\n","\n","*   (1) Randomly simulate the time to the next event;\n","\n","*   (2) Given an event has occurred randomly select which event has occured\n","\n","3.   **Update** - based on 2. move the model time forward to the event time and update the state of the system\n","\n","4.   **Repeat** - Iterate through steps 2. and 3. until some stopping criteria is met\n","\n","This essentially follows our intuition and there is no \"technical trickery\" such as fancy sampling methods, acceptance/rejection, etc. However, due to the random nature of any trajectory we will have to loop over these steps multiple times to find \"typical\" reaction paths (or whatever property we are trying to study)."],"metadata":{"id":"u0MnO-QdcIRy"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Now, let's try to put the various steps together."],"metadata":{"id":"xHj7p9dDlt5S"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# number of steps in a Gillespie simulation\n","steps = 200   # Number of reactions\n","\n","# Set up holder arrays\n","T = np.zeros((steps+1))\n","N_A = np.zeros((steps+1))\n","N_B = np.zeros((steps+1))\n","N_AB = np.zeros((steps+1))\n","\n","# Store initial conditions\n","N_A[0] = N_A0\n","N_B[0] = N_B0\n","N_AB[0] = N_AB0\n","\n","###### Main Code Loop ######\n","for j in range(steps):\n","    # Calculate updated overall reaction rate\n","    R = kf * N_A[j] * N_B[j] + kb * N_AB[j]\n","\n","    # Calculate time to next reaction\n","    u1 = np.random.random()\n","    tau = 1/R * np.log(1/u1)\n","\n","    # Store reaction time\n","    T[j+1] = T[j] + tau\n","\n","    # Select which reaction to occur\n","    Rf = kf * N_A[j] * N_B[j] / R\n","    u2 = np.random.random()\n","\n","    # Update populations\n","    if u2 < Rf:\n","        N_A[j+1] = N_A[j] - 1\n","        N_B[j+1] = N_B[j] - 1\n","        N_AB[j+1] = N_AB[j] + 1\n","    else:\n","        N_A[j+1] = N_A[j] + 1\n","        N_B[j+1] = N_B[j] + 1\n","        N_AB[j+1] = N_AB[j] - 1\n"],"metadata":{"id":"KEG9tmuwNOtm"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Let's try to plot what the trajectory looks like."],"metadata":{"id":"PcgwuTqRNoJP"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Set up plots\n","plots = [bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of A molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule A'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of B molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule B'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of AB molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule AB')]\n","\n","# Plot numbers for each molecule\n","plots[0].line(T, N_A, line_width=1,\n","                      alpha=1, line_join='bevel')\n","plots[1].line(T, N_B, line_width=1,\n","                      alpha=1, line_join='bevel')\n","plots[2].line(T, N_AB, line_width=1,\n","                      alpha=1, line_join='bevel')\n","\n","bokeh.io.show(bokeh.layouts.gridplot(plots, ncols=3))"],"metadata":{"id":"vdW0MHQVOJVd"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Practice Problem:** Try to run the trajectory several times. Do the changes in the number of molecules look the same each time?"],"metadata":{"id":"y5Zmu_VxOiv1"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Averaging the paths ###\n","\n","You might have found that we have a different answer each time we sample. Then what is the correct answer? Well, they are all possible ways that the molecule numbers can change. When we have just a small number of molecules (such as in many biological reactions), each time the reaction might happen differently. But if we allow the reaction to happen many times, we will observe something on average. Here, we do the reaction for many cycles and get the average trend.\n","\n","How about if we have a beaker of molecules A and B, with numbers on the order of $10^{23}$? With such a large number, you will surely observe the average trend!\n","\n","Now, let's do the simualtion many times, and find the average trend.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"9m4DBPKoPNIH"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Parameters for how many times to repeat the simulation\n","\n","steps = 200   # Number of reactions per trajectory\n","cycles = 100  # Number of trajectories iterated over\n","\n","# Set up holder arrays\n","T = np.zeros((cycles, steps+1))\n","N_A = np.zeros((cycles, steps+1))\n","N_B = np.zeros((cycles, steps+1))\n","N_AB = np.zeros((cycles, steps+1))\n","\n","# Store initial conditions\n","N_A[:,0] = N_A0\n","N_B[:,0] = N_B0\n","N_AB[:,0] = N_AB0\n","\n","###### Main Code Loop ######\n","for i in range(cycles):\n","    for j in range(steps):\n","        # Calculate updated overall reaction rate\n","        R = kf * N_A[i,j] * N_B[i,j] + kb * N_AB[i,j]\n","\n","        # Calculate time to next reaction\n","        u1 = np.random.random()\n","        tau = 1/R * np.log(1/u1)\n","\n","        # Store reaction time\n","        T[i, j+1] = T[i,j] + tau\n","\n","        # Select which reaction to occur\n","        Rf = kf * N_A[i,j] * N_B[i,j] / R\n","        u2 = np.random.random()\n","\n","        # Update populations\n","        if u2 < Rf:\n","            N_A[i,j+1] = N_A[i,j] - 1\n","            N_B[i,j+1] = N_B[i,j] - 1\n","            N_AB[i,j+1] = N_AB[i,j] + 1\n","        else:\n","            N_A[i,j+1] = N_A[i,j] + 1\n","            N_B[i,j+1] = N_B[i,j] + 1\n","            N_AB[i,j+1] = N_AB[i,j] - 1\n"],"metadata":{"id":"eHsZJsFkNoei"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Set up plots\n","plots = [bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of A molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule A'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of B molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule B'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of AB molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule AB')]\n","\n","# Plot trajectories and mean\n","for i in range(cycles):\n","  plots[0].line(T[i,:], N_A[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","  plots[1].line(T[i,:], N_B[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","  plots[2].line(T[i,:], N_AB[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","\n","\n","# Link axes\n","plots[0].x_range = plots[1].x_range\n","plots[2].x_range = plots[1].x_range\n","\n","bokeh.io.show(bokeh.layouts.gridplot(plots, ncols=3))"],"metadata":{"id":"4IA3PA4sQalT"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["There look like some average behaviors! Let's try to find these averages:"],"metadata":{"id":"2Teg7F_5Qf9b"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Calculate an average trajectory plot\n","ave_steps = 100\n","T_max = T.max()\n","\n","# Set up average arrays\n","T_ave = np.linspace(0,T_max,ave_steps+1)\n","N_A_ave = np.zeros(ave_steps+1)\n","N_B_ave = np.zeros(ave_steps+1)\n","N_AB_ave = np.zeros(ave_steps+1)\n","\n","N_A_ave[0] = N_A0\n","N_B_ave[0] = N_B0\n","N_AB_ave[0] = N_AB0\n","\n","# Pass over average array entries\n","for i in range(1, ave_steps+1):\n","    tmax = T_ave[i]\n","    A_sum = 0\n","    B_sum = 0\n","    AB_sum = 0\n","    t_count = 0\n","\n","    # Pass over each trajectory and step therein\n","    for j in range(cycles):\n","        for k in range(steps):\n","            if T[j,k] <= tmax and T[j,k+1] > tmax:\n","                t_count += 1\n","                A_sum += N_A[j,k]\n","                B_sum += N_B[j,k]\n","                AB_sum += N_AB[j,k]\n","\n","    # Caclulate average - taking care if no samples observed\n","    if t_count == 0:\n","        N_A_ave[i] = N_A_ave[i-1]\n","        N_B_ave[i] = N_B_ave[i-1]\n","        N_AB_ave[i] = N_AB_ave[i-1]\n","    else:\n","        N_A_ave[i] = A_sum / t_count\n","        N_B_ave[i] = B_sum / t_count\n","        N_AB_ave[i] = AB_sum / t_count"],"metadata":{"id":"y-PpcEYYmjuq"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":[" For visualization, we will plot every trajectory as a thin blue line, and then the average of the trajectories as a thick orange line."],"metadata":{"id":"S2377aByJX20"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Set up plots\n","plots = [bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of A molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule A'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of B molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule B'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               x_axis_label='time',\n","                               y_axis_label='number of AB molecules',\n","                               title = 'Molecule AB')]\n","\n","# Plot trajectories and mean\n","for i in range(cycles):\n","  plots[0].line(T[i,:], N_A[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","  plots[1].line(T[i,:], N_B[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","  plots[2].line(T[i,:], N_AB[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","\n","# Plot the averages\n","plots[0].line(T_ave, N_A_ave,\n","                  line_width=3, color='orange', line_join='bevel')\n","plots[1].line(T_ave, N_B_ave,\n","                  line_width=3, color='orange', line_join='bevel')\n","plots[2].line(T_ave, N_AB_ave,\n","                  line_width=3, color='orange', line_join='bevel')\n","\n","# Link axes\n","plots[0].x_range = plots[1].x_range\n","plots[2].x_range = plots[1].x_range\n","\n","bokeh.io.show(bokeh.layouts.gridplot(plots, ncols=3))"],"metadata":{"id":"aj-8jkAHnKbf"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["We can also compute the steady state properties by considering the end of the simulation. The last 50 time points are at steady state, so we will average over them."],"metadata":{"id":"ulMYFmBOJ3O0"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["print('mean number of A =', N_A[:,-50:].mean())\n","print('mean number of B =', N_B[:,-50:].mean())\n","print('mean number of AB =', N_AB[:,-50:].mean())\n","print('variance in number of A =', N_A[:,-50:].std()**2)\n","print('variance innumber of B =', N_B[:,-50:].std()**2)\n","print('variance innumber of AB =', N_AB[:,-50:].std()**2)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"AnAgpgNWJ1GE","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1711750200285,"user_tz":420,"elapsed":178,"user":{"displayName":"Bilin Zhuang","userId":"10579775607219082948"}},"outputId":"e042e891-1d1f-4a1f-adef-b9cd0bc6bb4f"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["mean number of A = 1.64\n","mean number of B = 11.64\n","mean number of AB = 28.36\n","variance in number of A = 1.2464000000000002\n","variance innumber of B = 1.2464000000000002\n","variance innumber of AB = 1.2464000000000002\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["It may also be interesting to look at how the number of one molecule change with the number of another molecule. Here, we can make a scatter plot to see the trend."],"metadata":{"id":"W4RuB3ibJcY0"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Set up plots\n","plots = [bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               y_axis_label='number of AB molecules',\n","                               x_axis_label='number of A molecules'),\n","         bokeh.plotting.figure(width=300,\n","                               height=200,\n","                               y_axis_label='number of AB molecules',\n","                               x_axis_label='number of B molecules')]\n","\n","# Plot trajectories and mean\n","for i in range(cycles):\n","  plots[0].line(N_A[i,:], N_AB[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","  plots[1].line(N_B[i,:], N_AB[i,:], line_width=0.3,\n","                      alpha=0.2, line_join='bevel')\n","\n","bokeh.io.show(bokeh.layouts.gridplot(plots, ncols=2))"],"metadata":{"id":"Zmt5JPH5JpOT"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## Another simple reaction\n","\n","Let's now consider another simple reaction, the *isomerization reaction*. There are many examples of such an reaction, for example, in the cracking process used in the petrochemical industry, n-butane can turn into i-butane.\n","\n","![](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/622d79724183683c3cb02a569ea1758f5fc53b58)\n","\n","The reaction can be illustrated as\n","$$ A ⟺ B $$\n","\n","Let the rate constants for the forward and the backward reaction be $k_f$ and $k_b$ respectively. The rate of the forward reaction is then\n","\n","$$r_f = k_f N_A$$\n","\n","and the rate of the backward reaction is\n","\n","$$r_b = k_b N_B$$\n","\n","Simulate the reaction using the Gillespie Algorithm. How does the number of $A$ and $B$ change with time? How does this depend on the relative magnitude of $k_f$ and $k_b$?"],"metadata":{"id":"XtMWfEq9Q9Lw"}}]}